  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-89350",
    "knowledge_point_id": "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e",
    "knowledge_point": "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/50f094ee1f26d1d68672c354fc15305c18e48c6f2f97b797b45e8a228eef52b6.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$",
    "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/5516a6bbdb411a5f21bbf530ca93926c09f9fcfdb5d57a6947032d1525849560.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]$ and $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the right branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the right branch are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$",
